why 1 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The tune leaps up a fifth to A and the lower voices rise into I⁶ with it, each by its own interval.
why 2 0 LEAPS The alto climbs C♯–D–F♯–G into the fermata. The tritone C♯–G is spread over four notes and three chords.
why 4 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The D chord arrives on beat 4 and carries over the bar line while the soprano's G–F♯ resolves above it. The suspension, not a chord change, moves the music.
why 5 3/4 DOUBLING The tenor holds D over the bass's A as a 4–3 suspension that falls to C♯; the chord is V⁷ with a suspended fourth, so the ⁶₄ doubling rule does not apply.
why 7 3/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's step E–D toward the cadence; its doubling follows the upper voices.
why 8 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The cadential ⁶₄ of a cadence in A lands on the downbeat, as it should; the beat before only looked like the same chord. It resolves to E major and then to A at the fermata.
why 8 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tenor's G♯, leading tone of A, leaps up to C♯, the third of the A chord at the fermata. Rising to A would leave the chord without its third.
why 9 0 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to D while the tenor steps C♯–D, meeting in unison at the start of the phrase.
why 9 0 RANGE The bass starts the phrase high on D and drops an octave a beat later.
why 11 0 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto holds A over the E chord and falls to G♯: a 4–3 suspension, prepared, tied and resolved by step.
why 11 5/8 UNRESOLVED_NCT The tune holds E over the bass's D and falls to D: a 9–8 suspension, prepared, tied and resolved by step.
why 11 5/8 TENDENCY The alto's G♯ falls to F♯ as the harmony moves from E major to D over the bass's step C♯–D. It is part of a descending line, not a goal.
why 11 3/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's E on the way to the half cadence on A; its doubling follows the moving voices.
why 13 1/2 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both move up, D–G and A–E, so the tenor passes where the alto was. They move together, so the order stays clear.
